version: 3
metadata:
  name: ur5e-pick-place
  title: "UR5e pick-and-place: how accurate does the reported box pose have to be?"
  description: |
    A UR5e with a Robotiq 2F-85 is bolted to a workbench. A 60 mm box sits in front of it and an
    open bin stands to one side. Each trial: read the box's pose from a simulated detector, plan
    with MoveIt 2, pick the box up, carry it across and drop it in the bin. In roqsim (MuJoCo),
    over ROS 2.

    The factor is `position_stddev` -- the noise on the reported box pose. The question it asks is
    the one this cell is shaped by: the jaws open to about 87 mm around a 60 mm box, so there is
    ~13 mm of clearance PER SIDE, and that is the entire error budget for detection plus IK plus
    execution. The top level is expected to fail, which is the point -- an example that only ever
    passes shows you nothing about where the edge is.
  keywords: [robotics, manipulation, pick-and-place, moveit2, mujoco, roqsim, ur5e, robotiq,
             grasping, perception-noise]

configuration:
# Config-block names may not contain underscores, spaces or periods (schema rule), hence `noise`
# -- the FACTOR keeps its full name.
- name: noise
  variations:
  # A `sim:` override, not a scenario parameter: this is a property of the WORLD, and the roqsim
  # backend mounts the campaign's overrides as a document and hands the simulator `--override`.
  # So the sweep needs no plumbing through the scenario at all.
  #
  # The path is `components.<entity>.<plugin>.<key>`, and it was MEASURED rather than guessed:
  # `plugins.object_detector.position_stddev` and
  # `components.ur5e.components.object_detector.position_stddev` are both REFUSED here. An
  # unmatched override is refused outright, which is the good case -- the bad one is a path that
  # is accepted and lands nowhere.
  #
  # 0.0 is the control: it isolates how much of any failure is the grasp and the arm rather than
  # the perception.
  #
  # The upper levels were CALIBRATED by running them, not reasoned from the clearance. The first
  # sweep used [0, 4, 8, 14] mm on the theory that ~13 mm of clearance per side was the budget, and
  # the widest cell passed 3/3 -- because only the component ACROSS THE JAWS spends that budget,
  # and at 14 mm per axis it exceeds 13 mm only about a third of the time. A 31.7 mm error succeeded
  # in that sweep while a 21.7 mm one failed, which is why `detect_error_y_m` exists.
  #
  # So the levels are widened until failure is systematic rather than incidental: at 28 mm per axis
  # the across-jaw error exceeds the budget in roughly 3 runs in 5.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      sim: components.ur5e.object_detector.position_stddev
      values: [0.0, 0.008, 0.016, 0.028]

execution:
  # The robot description MoveIt plans against, DERIVED from the world the simulator loads rather
  # than committed beside it. `roqsim export moveit` writes all six files move_group needs -- the
  # URDF and its meshes, the SRDF with a sampled collision matrix, and the four YAMLs -- so nothing
  # generated is in this directory and a stale description cannot happen: the outputs are appended
  # to `run_files`, content-hashed into the configuration's identity, and skipped when nothing they
  # read has changed (the exporter's own `--manifest` reports what that was).
  #
  # `--root-link base` is NOT cosmetic. The detector reports in the MuJoCo body `base`, and that
  # string is also its published `frame_id`; the UR arms have no `base_link` body at all. Exporting
  # under the default `base_link` would leave detections and MoveIt's planning frame named
  # differently for the same frame, so every goal would need a transform that does not exist.
  #
  # `--tip-site pinch` ends the arm chain at the gripper's own grasp site instead of the flange.
  # Without it a goal for the fingertips is an offset from the flange, and every orientation
  # tolerance is multiplied by that lever arm.
  #
  # `image:` is SYMBOLIC, so this names no registry: the deployment decides which project serves
  # `robovast-roqsim`, and that is the image carrying both roqsim and the exporter.
  #
  # `inputs` names the whole `world/` directory rather than the world file alone, because the world
  # spawns a prop that lives beside it: the generator only stages what it is given, and a world whose
  # sibling was left behind fails on a missing model. The reference inside the world resolves against
  # the world's own directory wherever it is staged, so no mount and no working directory has to be
  # arranged for it -- the same file compiles here and in the simulator.
  generate:
  - shell:
      out: moveit/gen
      image: family:robovast-roqsim
      inputs: ["world"]
      command: >-
        bash -lc "source /ws/install/setup.bash &&
        roqsim export moveit --world {inputs[0]}/ur5e_pick_place.yaml --out {out}
        --name ur5e_2f85 --root-link base --tip-site pinch
        --manifest {out}/.generated.json --check"

  containers:
  # The simulator, in a container of its own, running roqsim's ordinary CLI -- the same command
  # that debugs this world by hand.
    simulation:
      backend: roqsim
      config: world/ur5e_pick_place.yaml
      # MEASURED over a full sweep rather than guessed, and left with headroom over the measurement.
      # The first version of this file reserved 9 cpu and 10Gi for the pod against a measured 2.25
      # and 1.1Gi -- which costs nothing on an idle lane and four times the throughput on a busy
      # one, on every job of every sweep. An example's reservations get copied, so they should be
      # the ones a reader ought to copy.
      resources:
        cpu: 1
        memory: 1Gi

  # The system under test: move_group and robot_state_publisher.
  #
  # `family:robovast-roqsim` rather than the default member, because MoveIt's SERVERS ship in the
  # roqsim flavour -- an arm cell is a roqsim cell, since what executes a MoveIt trajectory is
  # roqsim's own bridge. SYMBOLIC, so it still names no registry: which project serves it is the
  # deployment's business.
  #
  # There are no `system_packages` anywhere in this file, and that is the point of putting MoveIt
  # in the image: this campaign builds NO derived image, and the sut runs an image the node has
  # already pulled for the simulator.
    sut:
      image: family:robovast-roqsim
      # The most generous of the three: move_group's planning bursts are what this has to absorb.
      resources:
        cpu: 2
        memory: 1Gi

  # The trial and the measurement, nothing else. The DEFAULT image: `moveit_msgs` is in the base
  # now, and a MoveGroup client needs the messages, not the servers.
    scenario:
      resources:
        cpu: 1
        memory: 512Mi

  mode: ros2
  # 5 repetitions, and the first sweep is why it is not 3: the outcome is a coin weighted by the
  # noise draw, so 3 runs cannot separate a 30% failure rate from a 60% one. At 3 the widest cell
  # read 3/3 successes, which inverted the curve. 4x5 = 20 runs still finishes in minutes.
  runs: 5
  scenario_file: scenario.osc
  # Per-extension globs. NOT `files/*`: that pattern crosses `/` and sweeps `__pycache__/*.pyc`
  # into the configuration's identity hash, so a stale bytecode file changes what looks like the
  # same configuration.
  run_files:
  - "files/*.py"
  - "moveit/*.py"
  - "world/*.yaml"
  - "world/*.xml"
  # The generated description. Per-extension rather than `moveit/gen/*`, for the same reason as
  # above -- and the meshes are named separately because the URDF references them by path: without
  # them move_group loads a robot with NO collision geometry and plans straight through the bench,
  # which is a wrong answer rather than a crash.
  - "moveit/gen/*.urdf"
  - "moveit/gen/*.srdf"
  - "moveit/gen/*.yaml"
  - "moveit/gen/meshes/*.stl"
  # Generous, and it has to be: a trial is ~30 s of arm motion but move_group takes the better
  # part of a minute to come up (planners, kinematics plugin, the planning scene).
  timeout: 900
  # No `env:` at all. PYTHONUNBUFFERED is already exported by the entrypoint of every container a
  # campaign runs -- main and sidecar alike, whatever image it names -- so restating it here is dead
  # config that reads like a requirement. And no MUJOCO_GL, deliberately: which offscreen backend
  # works is a property of the node the simulator lands on, and roqsim picks it at startup.


# The box's own trajectory, so a failure can be READ rather than guessed at: `out.csv` says the box
# never rose, and this says where it was while the arm was busy not lifting it. `out.csv` itself
# needs no step here -- a CSV in the run directory is ingested as a table named after the file.
#
# `require:` makes a missing frame a loud failure instead of an empty table. The box is the frame
# the verdict is built from, so a run that recorded no box pose measured nothing at all.
results_processing:
  postprocessing:
  - rosbags_tf_to_csv:
      frames: all
      require: [graspable_box]

# Replay one run. Three processes have to be read together to see what happened -- the trial's
# phase, move_group's planner, and the simulator -- and the merged log is the only place they share
# a timeline. Without it a failure in any of them looks identical from outside: nothing moved.
visualization:
  results:
    run_view:
      panels:
      - scenario_tree:
          title: Trial
          position: {anchor: top-left, width: 320, height: "calc(50% - 44px)"}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: behaviors}
      - log:
          title: Trial, move_group and simulator
          position: {anchor: bottom, height: 240}
          minimizable: true
